n of safety -1098%), so there is no valuation cushion.","The fair-value models disagree (dispersion 36%), and the Monte Carlo range runs $9.53 to $37.41 (P10-P90), so treat the estimate as noisy.","On multiples it looks expensive against Real Estate peers: P/E 103.6 vs 23.9, EV/EBITDA 54.1 vs 16.2 and P/FCF 97.9 vs 17.8 (sector median).","Earnings yields are thin: an FCF yield of 1.0% (below the risk-free rate).","The 22 covering analysts target $264 on average, 8% above the price, well away from the model's fair value."],"prof":["ROIC of 2% falls short of the cost of capital of 7.7%, so growth at these returns destroys value; returns have swung a lot from year to year (CV 0.30).","It converts 17% of revenue into free cash flow, or 3% after stock compensation.","Earnings quality looks solid: cash conversion of 3.08x.","AFFO margin (a proxy for distributable REIT cash) is 17%.","ROE of 2% comes mostly from margins and asset turnover rather than leverage (1.6x equity multiplier).","It spends 14.4% of revenue on stock compensation (above the 2% gate)."],"hlth":["Leverage is heavy at 6.8x net debt to EBITDA ($5.0B of cash against $19.2B of debt), and interest cover is thin at 2.6x."],"growth":["Revenue has compounded at 22.7% over 3 years, 18.7% over 5 years and 10.9% over 10 years, so growth is accelerating, and it has been lumpy (year-to-year swings of 17%).","Net reinvestment is nil (capex and working capital no more than cover depreciation), so fundamental growth is near zero.","Free cash flow has compounded 10.3% a year over five years.","Margins are contracting (-1.6% trend).","Forward indicators: FFO growth of 16.1% a year over five years.","Capex runs only 0.5x depreciation (9.7% of sales), so the asset base is being harvested rather than grown."],"own":["Insiders hold little stock (0.1%), and reported institutional holdings exceed 100% of shares (a sign of heavy share lending).","Insiders have only bought over the past year (4 purchases, net $2.4M bought).","Sh